1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,939
Sam had asked for the usual creative brief: tone words, a mood board, something to

2
00:00:04,939 --> 00:00:05,890
hand a generator.

3
00:00:05,890 --> 00:00:10,311
Dana opened a blank doc, typed "brand voice: warm, confident, trustworthy," and

4
00:00:10,311 --> 00:00:10,758
stopped.

5
00:00:10,758 --> 00:00:12,997
None of that was actually true or false.

6
00:00:12,997 --> 00:00:17,473
None of it was something a generator, or Dana herself at midnight, could check a

7
00:00:17,473 --> 00:00:18,816
line of ad copy against.

8
00:00:18,816 --> 00:00:23,517
So she deleted it and wrote three things instead: what Harlan actually charges, what

9
00:00:23,517 --> 00:00:27,994
it actually costs to make and ship a pair, and the one claim about the socks she

10
00:00:27,994 --> 00:00:30,736
will never let ship, no matter how well it tests.

11
00:00:30,736 --> 00:00:35,268
That short page has a name: the product truth document, the page that says what's

12
00:00:35,268 --> 00:00:38,682
provably true about a product, not what sounds good about it.

13
00:00:38,682 --> 00:00:42,935
Its whole job is answering one question: what can this brand actually prove?

14
00:00:42,935 --> 00:00:44,725
Notice what that question isn't.

15
00:00:44,725 --> 00:00:49,090
It isn't "what sounds good", and it isn't "what would a competitor say." Those

16
00:00:49,090 --> 00:00:51,496
questions come later, and they come easily.

17
00:00:51,496 --> 00:00:56,197
This one comes first because it's the only question a generator, a copywriter, or an

18
00:00:56,197 --> 00:01:00,114
entire batch of twenty variants can be checked against after the fact.

19
00:01:00,664 --> 00:01:03,501
Here's Harlan's, in full, and notice how short it is.

20
00:01:03,501 --> 00:01:04,839
That's not a first draft.

21
00:01:04,839 --> 00:01:06,391
That's the finished document.

22
00:01:06,391 --> 00:01:10,834
The price and the spec are just facts, copied over from what you already know about

23
00:01:10,834 --> 00:01:11,476
the account.

24
00:01:11,476 --> 00:01:14,045
The interesting part is the bottom two sections.

25
00:01:14,045 --> 00:01:16,775
One lists exactly what the brand is allowed to say.

26
00:01:16,775 --> 00:01:21,218
The other lists exactly what it isn't, and that second list exists because Dana has

27
00:01:21,218 --> 00:01:25,714
watched people overclaim products far less regulated than a compression sock and pay

28
00:01:25,714 --> 00:01:26,410
for it later.

29
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:30,852
Read the "what we can claim" section again, and notice how unglamorous it is on its

30
00:01:30,852 --> 00:01:31,067
own.

31
00:01:31,067 --> 00:01:32,298
Neither line is a hook.

32
00:01:32,298 --> 00:01:33,529
Neither is meant to be.

33
00:01:33,529 --> 00:01:35,991
This document isn't trying to stop the scroll.

34
00:01:35,991 --> 00:01:40,380
It's trying to make sure whatever does stop the scroll, written later, doesn't say

35
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:43,538
anything Dana can't stand behind if someone screenshots it.

36
00:01:44,088 --> 00:01:48,294
It's worth being strict about what stays out of this document, because the

37
00:01:48,294 --> 00:01:50,795
temptation is to let it become a mood board.

38
00:01:50,795 --> 00:01:55,286
Tone, angle ideas (a specific product story you could test), hooks, all of that

39
00:01:55,286 --> 00:01:58,355
belongs later, once you're actually briefing creative.

40
00:01:58,355 --> 00:02:02,732
If you let those in here, you dilute the one job this document has: being the

41
00:02:02,732 --> 00:02:05,631
fact-check every later claim gets measured against.

42
00:02:05,631 --> 00:02:06,824
An angle can be bold.

43
00:02:06,824 --> 00:02:09,837
A fact either survives Dana reading it or it doesn't.

44
00:02:09,837 --> 00:02:12,054
This is also why the document is short.

45
00:02:12,054 --> 00:02:16,714
If it ran to three pages, it would start accumulating opinions disguised as facts,

46
00:02:16,714 --> 00:02:19,670
and opinions are exactly what it exists to keep out.

47
00:02:19,670 --> 00:02:24,388
A page, or less, forces the discipline: only things that are actually true make the

48
00:02:24,388 --> 00:02:24,615
cut.

49
00:02:25,165 --> 00:02:29,775
Here's the line from the code slide, made concrete with actual sentences, because

50
00:02:29,775 --> 00:02:34,384
"spec versus outcome" is easy to agree with in the abstract and easy to miss in a

51
00:02:34,384 --> 00:02:35,010
real draft.

52
00:02:35,010 --> 00:02:37,286
The first two rows describe the product.

53
00:02:37,286 --> 00:02:42,066
They're checkable against the spec sheet, and they stay true no matter who's reading

54
00:02:42,066 --> 00:02:42,350
them.

55
00:02:42,350 --> 00:02:47,130
The bottom three describe a result in someone's body, or borrow a credibility Harlan

56
00:02:47,130 --> 00:02:47,927
hasn't earned.

57
00:02:47,927 --> 00:02:52,479
Notice that all five sentences could plausibly come out of a generator asked for

58
00:02:52,479 --> 00:02:57,031
"persuasive copy about compression socks." Only two of them are Harlan's to say.

59
00:02:57,581 --> 00:03:01,959
Let's be precise about why the boundary matters, because "don't overclaim" sounds

60
00:03:01,959 --> 00:03:06,444
obvious until you're the one under deadline pressure, staring at a draft that would

61
00:03:06,444 --> 00:03:09,092
perform better with one small exaggeration in it.

62
00:03:09,092 --> 00:03:13,469
A claims boundary is a line drawn in advance, by the person who has to answer for

63
00:03:13,469 --> 00:03:15,198
it, before that pressure exists.

64
00:03:15,198 --> 00:03:18,225
For Harlan, the line runs between a spec and an outcome.

65
00:03:18,225 --> 00:03:22,602
Fifteen to twenty millimetres of mercury of graduated compression is a fact about

66
00:03:22,602 --> 00:03:23,251
the product.

67
00:03:23,251 --> 00:03:27,790
Reducing swelling is a claim about somebody's body, and that's a promise Dana hasn't

68
00:03:27,790 --> 00:03:29,141
earned the right to make.

69
00:03:29,141 --> 00:03:33,572
You'll see exactly what happens when a draft crosses that line in Section six, and

70
00:03:33,572 --> 00:03:35,626
it isn't a small problem when it does.

71
00:03:35,626 --> 00:03:39,625
Why draw the line at "outcome" specifically, rather than somewhere looser?

72
00:03:39,625 --> 00:03:43,840
Because an outcome claim is the one kind of statement a generator will produce

73
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:45,893
confidently and completely unprompted.

74
00:03:45,893 --> 00:03:50,379
Ask for persuasive copy about a compression sock and a health-outcome sentence is a

75
00:03:50,379 --> 00:03:52,703
natural, fluent thing for a model to write.

76
00:03:52,703 --> 00:03:53,945
It isn't lying exactly.

77
00:03:53,945 --> 00:03:58,269
It's doing what persuasive copy about health products has always tended to sound

78
00:03:58,269 --> 00:04:02,700
like, without knowing that this specific brand hasn't run the study to back it up.

79
00:04:03,250 --> 00:04:07,759
I want to be honest about what this costs you, because a real claims boundary does

80
00:04:07,759 --> 00:04:08,584
cost something.

81
00:04:08,584 --> 00:04:13,038
Some of the most persuasive lines a generator will hand you will also be the ones

82
00:04:13,038 --> 00:04:14,687
that cross the line Dana drew.

83
00:04:14,687 --> 00:04:19,196
"Doctor recommended." "Clinically proven." Cutting those isn't caution for its own

84
00:04:19,196 --> 00:04:19,471
sake.

85
00:04:19,471 --> 00:04:24,090
Every one of them is a liability wearing a good headline, and the account that keeps

86
00:04:24,090 --> 00:04:28,599
one in because it tested well is one platform review away from losing the whole ad

87
00:04:28,599 --> 00:04:30,028
account, not just that ad.

88
00:04:30,028 --> 00:04:32,338
That trade only feels bad in the abstract.

89
00:04:32,338 --> 00:04:36,902
In practice, the variants that survive the boundary still have plenty to work with:

90
00:04:36,902 --> 00:04:39,761
a real spec number, a real audience, a real problem.

91
00:04:39,761 --> 00:04:41,685
You're not trading away persuasion.

92
00:04:41,685 --> 00:04:46,139
You're trading away the specific persuasion that was never actually yours to use.

93
00:04:46,689 --> 00:04:51,360
Next lecture stays close to the same document, but turns it outward: not what Harlan

94
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:55,919
can say, but who's actually listening, and what they already believe before the ad

95
00:04:55,919 --> 00:04:56,586
even starts.

96
00:04:56,586 --> 00:05:01,257
Keep the product truth doc open while you read it, because the next few lectures all

97
00:05:01,257 --> 00:05:02,313
reach back into it.

98
00:05:02,313 --> 00:05:06,983
Everything from here is really the same document, viewed from a different angle each

99
00:05:06,983 --> 00:05:07,261
time.

100
00:05:07,261 --> 00:05:11,654
Before you write anything else this week, get your own three lines down: price,

101
00:05:11,654 --> 00:05:15,824
margin, and the one claim you would not want to defend under bright lights.

102
00:05:15,824 --> 00:05:20,439
That's page one of the document the workshop in 2.6 turns into artifact B01, and it

103
00:05:20,439 --> 00:05:24,998
takes fifteen minutes if you don't let yourself overthink the claims boundary yet.
